Runtime waits, quotas, buses and base-tool expansion (2026-10-13)
================================================================

30 new probes: 17 kernel and 13 userland, covering the eight areas requested
following COVERAGE-FIFTH. coverage-sixth.json records exact types, source
hashes and profile names. Cron already had job-run/job-done through wrapper
macros; this round extends that provider rather than duplicating those events.

Placement and lifetime
----------------------

The restrictions in COVERAGE-FIFTH still apply. No sites were added to early
bootstrap, DTrace internals, trap/NMI/IPI entry, low-level interrupt handlers,
bit-banging, scheduler primitives, debugger or panic entry. Shared runtime
routines may also run during initialization, but pre-enable execution is not
recorded. Disabled kernel sites are NOPs; no-hooks builds remove the probes.
The source must not call DTrace's fallback stub before provider enablement.

Socket and buffer-object waits use existing caller locks and lifetimes; the
completion sites do not dereference their identity arguments. Quota decision
sites execute under the existing dquot interlock, before warning paths unlock
it. Quota writeback retains the existing DQ_LOCK protection across VOP_WRITE.
MMC requests remain owned by the waiting caller until its completion flag is
observed; neither the bridge interrupt handler nor mmc_wakeup is instrumented.
I2C uses the existing bus/device lifetime rules at acquisition and synchronous
transfer boundaries. No probe arguments traverse message buffers. The MMC provider is defined in
the common mmc_subr.c so configurations do not depend on the legacy bus.

No probe adds allocation, sleeps, locks, payload copying, or retained object
references. Enabling probes still adds execution time and can affect timing.
The amd64 build/patchpoint checks are not architecture-wide or hardware safety
qualification. MMC and I2C controllers need live workload testing separately.

Kernel contracts (arguments in order)
-------------------------------------

sockbuf:::wait-start / wait-done
  start(socket, which); done(socket, which, sleep result).
  Around msleep_sbt in sbwait(), after setting SB_WAIT. 'which' is the native
  SO_SND/SO_RCV selector. Captures data-arrival/output-space sleep, timeouts
  and interruptions; not the complete socket operation or every socket wait.
  Waking successfully does not prove the caller's readiness condition holds.

bufcache:::writes-wait-start / writes-wait-done
  start(bufobj, initial outstanding-output count); done(bufobj, sleep result).
  Entire bufobj_wwait() loop, including immediate returns when there are no
  outstanding writes. Existing lock assertions precede the start. A successful
  wait means outstanding output drained, not that every write succeeded or
  data is durable. Individual write errors are not returned by this helper.

ufsquota:::deny
  (dquot, quota class, resource, reason).
  Quota class is native USRQUOTA/GRPQUOTA. Resource 0 = blocks, 1 = inodes;
  reason 0 = hard limit, 1 = expired soft-limit grace period. Emitted once
  before each denial path, regardless of whether a user warning is suppressed.
  Only UFS chkdqchg/chkiqchg decisions; privileged bypasses and other filesystems
  are not covered. This is an EDQUOT decision, not a generic storage failure.
ufsquota:::grace-start
  (dquot, quota class, resource, grace deadline in epoch seconds).
  On assignment of a new deadline after crossing the soft limit. Expiry is
  observed at the later deny event, not by a new timer callback.
ufsquota:::sync-start / sync-done
  start(dquot, quota class); done(dquot, errno).
  Actual quota-file VOP_WRITE and short-write-to-EIO conversion in dqsync().
  Excludes earlier locks/waits, clean-record skips and later vnode cleanup.
  Zero is the write-call result; it is not a stable-storage guarantee.

mmc:::request-start / request-done
  start(bus device, request, opcode); done(request, native MMC command error).
  The legacy MMC bus's mmc_wait_for_req(), from bridge submission through
  MMC_REQ_DONE observation. Excludes earlier retuning and later caller work.
  Return values are MMC_ERR_* values, not errno; the helper itself returns 0
  even when the command reports an error. No data, response or command-address
  payload is captured. CAM-based MMC paths that bypass this bus are not covered.
mmc:::retune-start / retune-done
  start(requesting device, reset-requested boolean); done(device, MMC result).
  The mmc_retune() call in mmc_wait_for_request(), before the caller translates
  or tolerates particular results. Nested command requests may occur inside it.
mmc:::command-retry
  (requesting device, opcode, MMC result, retries remaining before decrement).
  Failed attempts in mmc_wait_for_cmd() only when another attempt is allowed.
  Excludes terminal failures and the separate application-command retry loop.

 iicbus:::acquire-start / acquire-done
  start(bus, requesting device, flags); done(bus, requesting device, result).
  iicbus_request_bus() includes ownership contention and controller acquisition.
  Results retain the native IIC_* / IIC_ERRNO representation, not plain errno.
 iicbus:::transfer-start / transfer-done
  start(bus, message count); done(bus, native IIC result).
  IICBUS_TRANSFER callbacks from iicbus_transfer() and iicbus_transfer_excl().
  Acquisition failures produce no transfer pair. Release follows completion
  and is excluded. Nested wrappers can produce nested pairs; these are callback
  counts, not guaranteed counts of physical transactions. Direct driver calls
  outside these wrappers are not covered. No bus addresses or data are exposed.

Base-tool contracts
-------------------

New helper macros use generated typed provider headers and ENABLED guards;
arguments are discarded when tracing is compiled out. Enabled helper calls
save/restore errno so they do not corrupt subsequent error handling.
No probes were added to signal handlers or the mv vfork child.

 ddio*:::read-start / read-done; write-start / write-done
  start(fd, requested bytes); done(fd, signed syscall result, errno-or-zero).
  Actual read/write calls in dd_in/dd_out, before conversion/accounting and
  before after_io() processes pending termination. Includes partial results,
  EOF (read result 0), errors (-1), and write retries after EINTR. A zero write
  has errno 0 but dd treats it as end-of-device failure: examine both result
  and error. These do not cover setup/positioning reads or close/fsync phases.
 ddio*:::sparse
  (output fd, bytes deferred). A zero block was deferred instead of written;
  this does NOT mean a later seek or final file extension succeeded.

 moveio*:::rename-start / rename-done
  start(); done(native rename result, errno-or-zero).
  After overwrite prompts and before fallback handling. A successful rename
  is not followed by a fallback pair; precheck skips have neither pair.
 moveio*:::fallback-start / fallback-done
  start(regular-file boolean); done(mv helper result: 0 success, 1 failure).
  After EXDEV handling and source validation, around fastcopy() or copy(),
  including removal of the source when successful. Helper result is NOT errno.
  Metadata warnings can coexist with result 0. The external cp/rm implementation
  stays unchanged. Paths and file contents are not probe arguments.

 cron*:::job-launch
  (command-child pid or -1, requested uid, fork errno-or-zero).
  Emitted in the middle process for the fork that creates the command child.
  Successful fork does not imply successful credentials, setup or exec.
  Excludes the daemon's initial fork and failures before command-child creation.
 cron*:::job-exec-error
  (errno), immediately after the command-shell execl returns. It is an event
  in the command child, with its pid supplied by DTrace's pid builtin.
 cron*:::child-wait-start / child-wait-done
  start(requested child pid);
  done(requested child pid, wait return pid, raw wait status, errno-or-zero).
  One pair across EINTR retries in wait_on_child(). On failure, status is 0
  as a placeholder and must not be interpreted as success; consult errno/return
  pid. Includes command and stdin-helper waits. This is reap-wait latency,
  NOT full job runtime: output processing may have consumed most of that time.
  Cross-process launch/completion correlation uses recorded child pids, not tid;
  PID reuse, fork/exec discovery and event loss require care in live consumers.

Cron's existing job-run/job-done names and argument contracts are preserved.
Their wrappers now use the generated provider header too: mixing its typed
prototypes with the previous generic DTRACE_PROBE macros caused incompatible
symbol declarations. The old events remain present in linked DOF, with
explicit enable guards. No job commands, credentials or execution policy were
changed, and no scheduler configuration was modified.

Installed base integration
--------------------------

  /bin/dd, /bin/mv       runtime package
  /usr/sbin/cron         cron package

Providers are compiled with MK_DTRACE=yes, omitted with MK_DTRACE=no. mv now
explicitly lists mv.c in SRCS because adding a provider .d otherwise replaces
the implicit single-source default. Cron extends its existing provider .d.
No extra installed binary or runtime dependency is added.

The existing bsdinstruments profile selection installs eight new profiles in
/usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles, in the bsdinstruments package:

  socket-buffer-waits   quota-decisions       mmc-requests
  iicbus-transfers      buffer-write-waits    dd-transfers
  move-fallbacks        cron-child-results

The working-tree catalog has 331 entries after this round. Installation
selection was checked; no package publication or installation was performed.
Profiles aggregate native result codes, not a universal errno namespace.
The dd profile also counts actual bytes, zero results, and deferred sparse
bytes. Cron reports raw wait statuses separately from wait errors.

Elapsed histograms include preemption/blocking and enabled-probe overhead.
Start before the workload. Per-thread depth handles synchronous nesting, not
lost events or process exits. PID filters select the executing process; workers
and cron children may not match a selected application's or daemon's pid.
Pointer identities are never dereferenced by these profiles.

Validation
----------

From /usr/src, with permission to write /usr/obj:

  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-compile.py \
    --kernel-obj /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-base.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-check.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-objects.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-dof.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-sixth-smoke.py

Artifacts, commands, logs, before-images and a session-only patch are in
/usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-sixth-validation. Parallel compilation uses every logical
CPU. Validation covers 14 kernel configurations (six sources, hooks on/off,
including UFS built-in and module variants),
three base programs in both variants, actual kernel patchpoint relocations
and inactive amd64 NOP bytes, linked userland DOF sites/enable guards, preserved
cron probes, and 16 plain/filtered profile compilations plus install selection.
The prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG kernel, mmc, iicbus and ufs modules were also linked.

33 functional build/run checks include dd short pipe reads, partial tails,
count=0, sync padding, sparse output, read/write errors; mv normal rename,
no-clobber, real cross-filesystem regular-file and directory/symlink fallback,
missing input and bad destinations; and cron's exact wait helper with normal,
signaled and interrupted child termination. The helper is extracted unchanged
into the harness to avoid linking unrelated job-launch/PAM/mail paths.
Cross-filesystem checks require /tmp and the object directory on distinct
mounts; the harness explicitly verifies this prerequisite rather than faking
EXDEV. All test files/child processes are isolated; cron itself is not started.

The host lacks /dev/dtrace. These checks do not establish live probe firing,
provider registration, enable/disable races, or hardware workload behavior.
MMC/I2C recovery injection, quota-enabled UFS workloads, socket wake/timeout
correlation and live consumer stress still require a booted test kernel.
No kernel/modules were loaded or installed, and no reboot was performed.
